\code{choose_period = TRUE} fits the model to the last years only, starting in
the year that minimises the ratio of the mean deviance of a fit with a
linear \code{k[t]} to the mean deviance of the Lee-Carter fit (Booth,
Maindonald and Smith 2002): the longest recent period over which the
decline was close to linear (\code{demography::lca(chooseperiod = TRUE, breakmethod = "bms")} gives the same period). Fitted rates of earlier
years are missing.

Compared with \code{demography::lca()}, \code{k[t]} is centred again after the
second stage (a constant shift of \code{k} and \code{a}, with identical fitted
rates), and life tables use \code{a_x = n/2} at age 0 (as \code{\link[=life_table]{life_table()}}), so
\code{adjust = "e0"} matches a slightly different life expectancy than
demography's life table where age 0 is in the data.

The period index follows a random walk with drift. Draws include the
innovations and the uncertainty of the drift. With \code{bootstrap = TRUE} the
parameters are re-estimated on \code{n_boot} Poisson resamples of the fitted
deaths of the fitting period (semi-parametric bootstrap with fitted deaths
of Renshaw and Haberman 2008), which adds the uncertainty of \code{a} and \code{b}.
The bootstrap of Brouhns, Denuit and Van Keilegom (2005) resamples the
observed deaths instead: \code{bootstrapped(lee_carter(), type = "data")}
(\code{\link[=bootstrapped]{bootstrapped()}}).

Age groups without deaths in the fitting period get a constant rate (0.5
deaths over the period) and no trend; with \code{adjust = "e0"} they enter the
life tables at that rate.

\strong{Safeguards of this package for sparse counts.} Where the Poisson
likelihood has no finite maximum (\code{method = "poisson"} with very few
